from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/87/8726ab8c3d764016e1bfa5206769674b6809ab3ea047b03734ae5141f0b553b9.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that if we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/56/56e77c37324ca2f28c0a09d762d1e5e1f4a29f5f00695e5cbadca748d92b1c6f.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186414786 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179742 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d72127a-90d6-5f7e-bd87-75d03285a918" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Non-Constant Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1643f0dc-f7e7-5dad-a5b8-aae4b765e7d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186414760} {:answer/id #uuid "68101330-8a2c-5b91-a587-3cdb07c9e267" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186414767} {:answer/id #uuid "945c7fb5-ffad-5ef7-8bdb-b1a3bf71f097" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186414771} {:answer/id #uuid "c1849629-2bb6-5ec1-9c2e-547f226eb5e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186414775} {:answer/id #uuid "fdbf4f19-642b-5af3-8c6c-71292db7385f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186414777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1643f0dc-f7e7-5dad-a5b8-aae4b765e7d9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186414760} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff707b18-5e56-5002-82ef-862a25939ae5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186414782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7554a362-8a42-5a06-a982-68a08488fe59" :question/math-academy-id "q-125687" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1ba55883d76b7e48148febcf6203a987575e78179c2fcde1963ab9db34a37738.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the сurve is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$  works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/e7/e783fb958f01da9552ef4258adf4bed1f7b083520ad9a59082b5479dc2fc7d40.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/20/20798c6992f536d4f280243746f8599bd4589bd69e253caeed65150b8a9944b7.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186415050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f567e86-63a5-512d-b830-be97bedcf0c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Division Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab1a91d-d33a-5e51-b220-e0e4da05d420" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186414931} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f6d41d-dc0c-54e9-864e-febae8295631" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186414985} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d03008-076d-5979-8ec2-d15997b802f9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186414986} {:answer/id #uuid "ee51c39b-f541-5db9-9b1d-bc0125830007" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186415009} {:answer/id #uuid "f95294ea-d39c-5f5e-8043-8a30f497210f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186415014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0f6d41d-dc0c-54e9-864e-febae8295631" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186414985} :answer-field/id #uuid "185bfed6-c6e9-5751-adee-92e27148dc54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186415021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cdd37720-ffc4-552d-8b81-f3aa2f866efb" :question/math-academy-id "q-103026" :question/problem "If \n$ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8)$\nis equivalent to\n$a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,8),$\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k?$\n\nI. $k=8$\n\nII. $k=-12$\n\nIII. $k=-15$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=8.$ Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=8,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(8,8)=8\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=8.$\n\n- If $k=-12,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-12,8)=4\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-12.$\n\n- If $k=-15,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-15,8)=1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=-15.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186415053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186152095 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f567e86-63a5-512d-b830-be97bedcf0c8" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Second Division Property"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d32cab0-a947-5df3-b52a-1e20d4a89706" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186414933} {:answer/id #uuid "19a4b986-70e3-5216-b16f-03a5fda6657d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186414939} {:answer/id #uuid "5a04721a-ae8a-5b6e-8878-aabea63fdaaa" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186414956} {:answer/id #uuid "636fa707-191d-5267-8fee-440385919afb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186414961} {:answer/id #uuid "a4b3e191-4f8f-5513-9308-e68abbfe8bcb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186414987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4b3e191-4f8f-5513-9308-e68abbfe8bcb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186414987} :answer-field/id #uuid "68b518f5-1d9e-5140-a83d-d1e076453bbe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186415029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "eaf9f361-8af6-5a67-abf5-86a0bbac7a1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103018" :question/problem "If \n$ka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10)$\nis equivalent to\n$a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,10),$\nwhich of the following could be the value of $k?$\n\nI. $k=12$\n\nII. $k=3$\n\nIII. $k=-5$" :question/worked-solution "The division property of modular arithmetic states that\n\n$$\nka≡kb\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,a≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\n$$\n\nprovided that $k$ and $n$ are coprime. In other words, we require $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=1.$\n\nIn this case, the modulus $n=10.$ Let's consider each value of $k$ in turn.\n\n- If $k=12,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(12,10)=2\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=12.$\n\n- If $k=3,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(3,10)=1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is valid for $k=3.$\n\n- If $k=-5,$ then $\\text{gcd}(k,n)=\\text{gcd}(-5,10)=5\\ne 1.$ Therefore, our equivalence is *not* valid for $k=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186415156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198449 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4a5d6b1-e76a-5b33-8973-04d1d9062dc6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Orthogonality of a Two-Vector Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fce5c53-c88f-5213-9b57-f76cc256d9ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186415150} {:answer/id #uuid "75a2a860-9b99-512b-901f-14c2d162a694" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186415151} {:answer/id #uuid "87e451be-c0a6-5f4f-8367-e5f2bd3d8579" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true." :db/id 17592186415152} {:answer/id #uuid "a91a4b12-8794-5dbc-b111-cafaa0420df7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186415153} {:answer/id #uuid "ac673688-1d30-5b45-8f99-0b4b41a43e4a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186415154}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac673688-1d30-5b45-8f99-0b4b41a43e4a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186415154} :answer-field/id #uuid "288b1ab6-6f15-58b3-8714-e1582f968b3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186415155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a7fbace-2464-5d79-be05-e6f28745c3de" :question/math-academy-id "q-178014" :question/problem "$$\n{u}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal" :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=12-12=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186415187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186198261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5642c9-3238-501d-b001-abe804dbf74b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186415164} {:answer/id #uuid "42236876-92a9-57f6-83c9-30fca5f7268d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186415169} {:answer/id #uuid "49d6bd5f-dc44-5b25-a899-60660d018a65" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186415170} {:answer/id #uuid "c9063210-0eaf-5f51-9f26-d06d56f1944e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186415178} {:answer/id #uuid "ead9dfa3-e4dc-59d6-a1e9-1696a9df2aa0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186415179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f5642c9-3238-501d-b001-abe804dbf74b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186415164} :answer-field/id #uuid "27e45ba6-a42f-5129-95cb-6948d10b75e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186415184}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d6a13d3-ad69-5578-b6a3-a3b3842749f5" :question/math-academy-id "q-183354" :question/problem "Consider the following predicates.\n\n$\\,$ $A(x):$ $x\\ge 2.$ $\\,$ $B(x):$ $-x\\le -2.$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the biconditional predicate $A(x)⇔B(x)?$\n\n- If $x\\ge 2$ then $-x\\le -2,$ and if $-x\\le -2$ then $x\\ge 2.$\n- $x\\ge 2$ is equivalent to $-x\\le -2.$\n- If $x\\ge 2$ then $-x\\le -2,$ or if $-x\\le -2$ then $x\\ge 2.$" :question/worked-solution "A biconditional expression $P⇔Q≡(P⇒Q)∧(Q⇒P)$ can be read as shown below:\n\n- $P$ if and only if $Q$\n\n- $P$ is equivalent to $Q$\n\n- If $P$ then $Q,$ and if $Q$ then $P$\n\nSo, $A⇔B$ translates into the following sentences:\n\n- $x\\ge 2$ if and only if $-x\\le -2.$\n\n- $x\\ge 2$ is equivalent to $-x\\le -2.$\n\n- If $x\\ge 2$ then $-x\\le -2,$ and if $-x\\le -2$ then $x\\ge 2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186415312 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179740 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f30993e-b5c7-5d8a-9b27-2aed2c9223e1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Sign of a Circulation From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11d3f253-290b-54bb-babf-a6a6ff8978c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186415283} {:answer/id #uuid "587cc8ba-51a2-5f70-b7ab-e29504848366" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186415289} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f13577-89cf-5b55-b7e4-ab887172e61d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186415296} {:answer/id #uuid "dad516a1-d6d5-51c7-9929-12667a17dd33" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186415300} {:answer/id #uuid "fe81a0b5-f0a2-514d-9d40-4d11839b169e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186415305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11d3f253-290b-54bb-babf-a6a6ff8978c0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186415283} :answer-field/id #uuid "01518b07-f415-5bbf-8b57-bf0d859e6135" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186415307}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e8f010b-1d85-5f55-981d-5803f1cc03c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-125645" :question/problem "![](images/98/981d966645eec322afbfe28a5a1f519eeab8b3557692dcce368a87fe748dddd6.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse $E$ and the vector field $F,$ shown above. Let $C$ be any path where $E$ is traversed once in the **clockwise** direction. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$" :question/worked-solution "The circulation $Γ$ measures the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along a *closed* curve $C.$\nIt is given by the formula\n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe now recall the following:\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement III is false. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves clockwise along $C.$ \n\n![](images/b2/b2473eb0c505d75d2c448c1a0d714d05d42737ce8d601401e925834a25ae9e14.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves counterclockwise along $C.$\n\n![](images/85/8513d9d15d204b6f63f310a37465d6b8dfb21239ca412bac7d3b9936f2bf5ebf.png)\n\nSo, $Γ={∮}_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186415314 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186179743 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1da220a9-2afc-5b63-a423-a4d2b3de9567" :knowledge-point/title "Circulation in Conservative Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e6bbb37-e5ee-59d8-9780-e4f18b4fd703" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id 17592186415286} {:answer/id #uuid "3daf5c5a-bb1b-59ca-aa2f-2855f76bd340" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186415287} {:answer/id #uuid "5d659d54-987d-5ce7-9597-87369b068528" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186415290} {:answer/id #uuid "68eb45d8-b286-53dc-b61c-d25ed72ae9e9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186415293} {:answer/id #uuid "e36e6492-906e-584d-9d76-b86aee6f54cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186415301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68eb45d8-b286-53dc-b61c-d25ed72ae9e9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186415293} :answer-field/id #uuid "340ffb7b-df1a-5614-b77d-8024725af8e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186415309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77e87c43-f6db-5082-9277-93ef32960dff" :question/math-academy-id "q-125659" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\operatorname{arctan}⁡2y\\,i+\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{1+4{y}^{2}}\\,j.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $F$ is **not** conservative\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any smooth path $C\\in {R}^{2}$\n- ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ for any ellipse $C\\in {R}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "If $F$ is a conservative vector field on ${R}^{2},$ then the circulation $Γ=0$ for any *closed* path $C\\in {R}^{2},$ that is \n\n$$\nΓ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\nWe notice that $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{4x}{1+4{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. The vector field $F$ is conservative since the partial derivatives are equal and continuous everywhere on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. The result $Γ=0$ applies to closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186415476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186133913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "abaeea03-6a2a-5d37-a2af-a9ea109175ad" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reasons Why a Determinant Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "143f48c0-a939-5a4c-b987-afdbbefec79a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186415447} {:answer/id #uuid "22066df8-6293-5194-a873-db4b694c6674" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186415450} {:answer/id #uuid "8e60b08a-cd17-575f-8815-735e23870208" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186415460} {:answer/id #uuid "b446fc5b-8c89-5d67-acf5-bbcf1be8249d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186415462} {:answer/id #uuid "fe594f39-ea49-5697-a493-c1ebeaf625b4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186415468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22066df8-6293-5194-a873-db4b694c6674" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186415450} :answer-field/id #uuid "44a7cadd-9704-50b9-80ee-df6bc66a727b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186415471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d883981-f066-5218-a1bf-dd76574de46e" :question/math-academy-id "q-42469" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns\n- $A$ has identical rows\n- $A$ has a zero row" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical rows.\n\n- $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2.05 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 6 & \\sqrt{7} & 2\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186415620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04206f72-e018-5925-a58f-179e897595dc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Existence of a Multiplicative Inverse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a41fc355-034f-5ded-bdfb-ebfb582a1bda" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186415595} {:answer/id #uuid "b03c9ec0-7f75-5d97-abbd-94b2869f47ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186415596} {:answer/id #uuid "b0c4fbb0-762c-5b67-afd1-1fb5e5110f8b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186415597} {:answer/id #uuid "d07425cc-ed1f-5b0a-8751-4e3973904462" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186415600} {:answer/id #uuid "d66363e6-df16-5391-b2d0-6a090267e3a9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186415601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d07425cc-ed1f-5b0a-8751-4e3973904462" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186415600} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0aef841-96f7-59bb-8f67-62f78c7ca5b1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186415612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6d34f2bd-1406-50c4-9338-867f1ecca34e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213405" :question/problem "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36?$\n\n- $5$\n- $13$\n- $24$" :question/worked-solution "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $5$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(5,36)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $13$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(13,36)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $24$ does *not* have a multiplicative inverse modulo $36$ since $\\text{gcd}(24,36)=12\\ne 1.\\,\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186415621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203912 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "04206f72-e018-5925-a58f-179e897595dc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Existence of a Multiplicative Inverse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "161735ec-56d4-5072-8a75-665310f43f19" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186415586} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5e7ff7-af02-5c83-9cc7-4a20cdd2b462" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186415587} {:answer/id #uuid "7aad8420-3c48-5474-98cb-d9e00f367a26" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186415592} {:answer/id #uuid "b51e64bf-376c-52bb-ac74-2778610f3e9f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186415598} {:answer/id #uuid "f3f743a1-b3bd-5c44-85bc-086b14acdf8d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186415603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aad8420-3c48-5474-98cb-d9e00f367a26" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186415592} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9802078-fa7f-52a5-a1e5-14125231ee58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186415610}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af0eb712-6f36-542d-b37c-e07c5fedf4a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-213407" :question/problem "Which of the following integers has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27?$\n\n- $11$\n- $16$\n- $23$" :question/worked-solution "An integer $a$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $n$ if and only if $a$ and $n$ are coprime, i.e., $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's examine the given options.\n\n- $11$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(11,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $16$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(16,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $23$ has a multiplicative inverse modulo $27$ since $\\text{gcd}(23,27)=1.\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186416064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "229aefd2-11b3-5163-b906-bbd86d424316" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186415996} {:answer/id #uuid "6bce8261-f995-5f77-9c0b-b77b795f8640" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186416014} {:answer/id #uuid "781adc97-4ef9-50db-8150-4fb68c77d192" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186416015} {:answer/id #uuid "e8456360-6cf3-5dbc-9b03-efff334fe8ec" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186416036} {:answer/id #uuid "fd84b5df-a423-5a5d-ad6d-2ba9d4d34b30" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186416043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "229aefd2-11b3-5163-b906-bbd86d424316" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186415996} :answer-field/id #uuid "16e4a9b9-ed3e-5143-9ca1-42dbee28c3b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186416048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "538c45af-5de6-56ca-85b6-7f902af26d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-213261" :question/problem "![](images/de/ded4e8a94730585809c5534fee8d401481c919d529a67cd088e96b8797f2b237.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{2,5,8\\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(2,2)$\n\nII. $(2,8)$\n\nIII. $(8,5)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $2$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(2,8)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $8.$\n\n- The pair $(8,5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $8$ and ends at the node $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186416067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1997f0c1-dbf6-5676-9a65-8a59d13b359b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186415994} {:answer/id #uuid "4f900224-55b2-5719-9556-cfc3c22bf31d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186416006} {:answer/id #uuid "cc702308-7c8f-52c1-8bba-64531db6e716" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186416030} {:answer/id #uuid "e38ff878-929d-5ee9-9b71-f303cbb883cc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186416034} {:answer/id #uuid "e84d09a8-9f4e-5918-a25a-eab0fa6840b0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186416037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1997f0c1-dbf6-5676-9a65-8a59d13b359b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186415994} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc10d4e-de6c-5af8-9374-56bcd531b20c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186416047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ba430a9-b9f3-5dc8-a610-c4d7d7129920" :question/math-academy-id "q-213262" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b23d63f453246fb606969ba651104097fca785324d5d0fd2203e176dda19fa5.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma \\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$\n\nII. $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$\n\nIII. $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair  $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $\\beta $ and ends at the node $\\gamma .$\n\n- The pair $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $\\gamma $ and ends at the node $\\alpha .$\n\n- The pair $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a an arrow that starts at the node $\\alpha $ and ends at the node $\\beta .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186416072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-e5d3ad7b6da2" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13272a5b-2de1-5012-9d4c-d0b6e9760b36" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186415992} {:answer/id #uuid "26c9b0be-0411-58d9-acc8-3ddc5b99d9ee" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186415997} {:answer/id #uuid "38c8fc2e-e74c-5869-91a3-f1b2979621f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186416002} {:answer/id #uuid "6905a4c0-8040-5b6a-87f7-803e19676be0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186416011} {:answer/id #uuid "edbf0707-8642-500c-bb4f-336aa28e52ad" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186416039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38c8fc2e-e74c-5869-91a3-f1b2979621f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186416002} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca8636af-9023-557c-9e61-5c98ec22e1da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186416059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e27df10f-2205-5c33-84a9-013636e28935" :question/math-academy-id "q-213167" :question/problem "![](images/35/35756183a7b4bf08baffb00d1f35dcc370b0e58831554daafa58d8f3d4359ab6.png)\n\nThe plot of the binary relation $S,$ defined over the set $X=\\{2,4,6\\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(2,2)$\n\nII. $(4,4)$\n\nIII. $(6,2)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(4,4)$ does not belong to the relation since the plot does not contain a point with such coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(6,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186416857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "306be17f-f950-57e2-9cb7-2b33c8a4929e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations Over Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab03ed4-fb9b-5074-9880-fe1c53b1877b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id 17592186416708} {:answer/id #uuid "2e52c3bd-2b7a-5a24-95b6-a56a7a15447c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id 17592186416713} {:answer/id #uuid "b4de6a26-5382-5b6d-bf0e-07b0e5da5b96" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id 17592186416780} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c5b715-7c1c-5058-8ab1-617e85ee6cba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id 17592186416795} {:answer/id #uuid "f7295677-3fca-59ea-b647-9db4f20d343a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $S$ only" :db/id 17592186416808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4de6a26-5382-5b6d-bf0e-07b0e5da5b96" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id 17592186416780} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7897328-67d4-5121-9241-5f901a3d93b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186416847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "653fba49-a6b9-5e57-b514-cf99f2424cd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-213246" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are reflexive? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡2y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $y≡x+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ defined on $Z$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is neither reflexive nor irreflexive. Notice that \n\n- $(1,1)\\notin R$ since $1≢2(1)\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7),$ while\n\n- $(7,7)\\in R$ since $7≡2(7)\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7).$\n\n- Relation $S$ is reflexive. Indeed, $(x,x)\\in S$ for all $x\\in Z.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,9)\n$$\n\nfor any $x\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is irreflexive. Indeed, $(x,x)\\notin T$ for all $x\\in Z.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≢x+2\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6)\n$$\n\nfor any $x\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186416858 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "306be17f-f950-57e2-9cb7-2b33c8a4929e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations Over Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21bdce35-1a6d-5b3f-a49e-4f39f1e7cd62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id 17592186416711} {:answer/id #uuid "58ae1950-b540-541c-bb35-734835ed6837" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id 17592186416731} {:answer/id #uuid "7c2cab9d-46e7-5fca-9e2e-839c1847e800" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id 17592186416750} {:answer/id #uuid "a67c2cc7-31d2-5884-b5e7-bd42e8198442" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id 17592186416774} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d8d161-c088-50a0-8cdb-b2dfab2b3cd6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $R$ only" :db/id 17592186416801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21bdce35-1a6d-5b3f-a49e-4f39f1e7cd62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id 17592186416711} :answer-field/id #uuid "a904c1ac-f48c-541f-87b2-64440b6f4699" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186416838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7095468b-6aec-5ce6-83d1-ea12403a6b5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-213240" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are reflexive? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x+y)∣10,$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $4∣({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $x∣(y+1),$ defined on $Z$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is neither reflexive nor irreflexive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,2)\\notin R$ since $2+2=4$ and $4∤10,$ while\n\n- $(1,1)\\in R$ since $1+1=2$ and $2∣10.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is reflexive. Indeed, $(x,x)\\in S$ for all $x\\in Z.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n4∣({x}^{2}-{x}^{2})\n$$\n\nfor any $x\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is neither reflexive nor irreflexive. Notice that \n\n- $(2,2)\\notin T$ since $2∤(2+1),$ while\n\n- $(1,1)\\in T$ since $1∣(1+1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$S$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186416863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4b47a31-7bda-5366-bac4-1d070776e776" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Reflexive and Irreflexive Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "003f486a-2600-584c-92ff-478adde52a81" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186416691} {:answer/id #uuid "4c56e2bb-43ed-5906-85e4-b291c9d37ae8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186416727} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2585bc-b01e-5f72-8bd0-21e0708314ed" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186416764} {:answer/id #uuid "a753fcea-864c-5d1d-9515-36b40da1b3f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186416775} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc8cdb8-d4be-5768-99b5-0263aa670d03" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186416785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a753fcea-864c-5d1d-9515-36b40da1b3f0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186416775} :answer-field/id #uuid "50c36a75-cd57-5a00-815a-7d34757ac50b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186416823}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e1a27c07-b36b-5525-ba6b-847b13ebf516" :question/math-academy-id "q-213228" :question/problem "![](images/03/03ddf06402ad38708d232cf28783c817bf881965d54650eb158e7ffca4ea76a2.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represent irreflexive relations defined on the set $A=\\{1,2,3\\}?$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is \n\n- *reflexive* if $(x,x)\\in R$ for all $x\\in A,$\n\n- *irreflexive* if $(x,x)\\notin R$ for all $x\\in A.$\n\nIf some pairs of the form $(x,x)$ belong to a relation and others do not, then the relation is neither reflexive nor irreflexive.\n\nSince $R$ must be irreflexive, we require \n\n$$\n(1,1)\\notin R,\\,(2,2)\\notin R,\\,(3,3)\\notin R.\n$$\n\nThese ordered pairs correspond to the diagonal points on the plots, as shown below:\n\n![](images/b6/b63d99459e8271e70ec36fd417d67565f3d5e77316815d49c235468e174aa1e9.png)\n\n \n\nA plot corresponding to an irreflexive relation must not contain diagonal points. Therefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186416924 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a14e359-8fcd-5956-94b7-cc4fc5609525" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186416874} {:answer/id #uuid "7e200172-34ca-5ab5-a0aa-e59cf6e4d94a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186416884} {:answer/id #uuid "8162f83a-7db2-5f3b-82a9-fd24f5cd3429" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186416886} {:answer/id #uuid "9432a7e4-d864-5909-96fb-bc5b42aaf049" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186416891} {:answer/id #uuid "d1f74fae-3121-59b5-8358-f435d8ddb989" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186416904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a14e359-8fcd-5956-94b7-cc4fc5609525" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186416874} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec0a627f-c1f6-5d47-8f41-058df8fd51e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186416922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "062c6773-1c0f-5106-b2b7-7c444f43389f" :question/math-academy-id "q-139820" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}|{X}_{i}|$\n\nII. $min({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{13},{X}_{15})$\n\nIII. $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15},\\sigma )$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only.\n\n- Expression II is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186416927 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80e7de96-4120-5130-bf63-a0d6c2259820" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of those listed" :db/id 17592186416885} {:answer/id #uuid "85f1e085-215f-55f0-b047-8e1844995119" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186416889} {:answer/id #uuid "ba0857c3-5c1b-52e2-a099-8d64c550ebdc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186416900} {:answer/id #uuid "daab6621-7a59-5df9-94ae-46892b25c2d3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186416905} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f31955-0b4d-58d4-8a01-1883943c46ab" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186416911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85f1e085-215f-55f0-b047-8e1844995119" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186416889} :answer-field/id #uuid "3688953c-5564-5430-b587-22b1cb1cae35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186416916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4893e2e1-c5c0-525e-90bd-45f0ee89d95b" :question/math-academy-id "q-143552" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ is unknown and $\\sigma $ is known, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $\\mu \\cdot \\sigma -\\text{min}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16})$\n- $\\mu -\\text{median}({X}_{2},{X}_{4},…,{X}_{16})$\n- $\\sigma -max({X}_{1},{X}_{3},…,{X}_{15})$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression.\n\n- Expressions I and II are *not* statistics. They are functions that contain the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression III is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ and the *known* population standard deviation $\\sigma $ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186416928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24978810-ea9d-557b-a6e4-ec60314cdf3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186416867} {:answer/id #uuid "312870f5-b5f4-5f45-a06d-0ca470866f05" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186416870} {:answer/id #uuid "363a8247-f9e4-5fde-b5a0-221b67b24dbd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186416871} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fa121b-5ca3-59a5-9ca2-3cfda696c7f3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186416907} {:answer/id #uuid "ea421d4e-7720-57f3-94e7-9b66a798936e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186416909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24978810-ea9d-557b-a6e4-ec60314cdf3b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186416867} :answer-field/id #uuid "d48c9bdb-925a-55e9-8810-2371ff4500d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186416920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ccfb8c1-3a4b-5435-8003-dc3e546488df" :question/math-academy-id "q-139813" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- $max({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$\n- $\\text{median}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9})$\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{9}{(\\frac{{X}_{i}-\\sigma }{\\mu })}^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expressions I and II are statistics. They are functions of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{9}$ only.\n\n- Expression III is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu $ and the unknown population standard deviation $\\sigma .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186416929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "111f2a1e-49a4-5425-9c30-77e8f10a7813" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186416866} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0c5de3-7c79-5b7e-a720-d262f7cd52f4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186416875} {:answer/id #uuid "4e974b83-28b6-5e89-995d-50a977968dc8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186416877} {:answer/id #uuid "4fbcabc8-67e3-58c7-ada3-e4560b3ebf7f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186416878} {:answer/id #uuid "7a32c857-a580-5328-90f1-f1b03c19902c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186416883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e974b83-28b6-5e89-995d-50a977968dc8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186416877} :answer-field/id #uuid "0717ebb9-5fd9-5b4c-acc5-d9a8ab6e92d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186416915}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b59e493b-eefe-51a6-8a6a-b1643795634d" :question/math-academy-id "q-139806" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\n- ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}$\n- $\\text{mode}({X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6})$\n- $\\frac{1}{3}({X}_{2}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{6})$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is *not* a statistic. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expression II is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ only.\n\n- Expression III is also a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186416931 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186188280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd3fd748-f238-59b2-badf-54b6e762836c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statistics"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f9b79d-3f80-5c8a-b293-cdd677cfaac1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186416864} {:answer/id #uuid "2f2aad8e-6939-56e2-9378-82654311c3d3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186416869} {:answer/id #uuid "be046d3d-00a0-5830-98b9-f8f3de43c605" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186416901} {:answer/id #uuid "c2e714c6-de29-55f1-9b71-cb080706ced6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186416902} {:answer/id #uuid "e85f5d4a-6136-5a58-a7ce-9cb33d65e0e7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186416908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be046d3d-00a0-5830-98b9-f8f3de43c605" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186416901} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1b91263-f5d8-543a-8a8c-c6fd7db5717b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186416919}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e079f787-5375-55b9-9502-e85783c5d546" :question/math-academy-id "q-139814" :question/problem "A sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ is drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ If $\\mu $ and $\\sigma $ are both unknown, which of the following are statistics?\n\nI. $({X}_{1}+{X}_{4}+{X}_{7})^{3}$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{({X}_{i}-\\mu )^{2}}{2}$\n\nIII. ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}\\frac{{X}_{i}^{2}+{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population. A statistic is a random variable expressed as a function of ${X}_{i}.$\n\nStatistics cannot involve unknown population parameters.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each expression. \n\n- Expression I is a statistic. It is a function of the sample ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{7}$ only. Note that a statistic doesn't need to include every sample element.\n\n- Expressions II is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population mean $\\mu .$\n\n- Expressions III is *not* statistics. It is a function that contains the unknown population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186416982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186208843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "352a7d2c-4aba-5246-8a20-ed79de007b95" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Matrix Expression Involving the Hadamard Product is Well-Defined"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "368e5c38-687c-5476-a766-53fe59679efb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186416956} {:answer/id #uuid "a2de33de-7c02-5cb9-b000-8fd4ffa0668b" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186416962} {:answer/id #uuid "a81f915c-2e4f-5036-ade2-5fb2255941e1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186416963} {:answer/id #uuid "a859c734-1278-56c3-b313-37ad8c178a6c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186416964} {:answer/id #uuid "cee12af1-4ad7-5e93-a79a-1c4026db489e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186416969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "368e5c38-687c-5476-a766-53fe59679efb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186416956} :answer-field/id #uuid "f680b101-94a2-558f-9708-756d2ba22da2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186416978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6deba07-cdc0-5243-9183-e0064a49692b" :question/math-academy-id "q-274938" :question/problem "Given the matrices\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\,Y=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\,u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n in which of the following expressions is the Hadamard product well-defined?\n\n- $YX⊙Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)$\n- $X⊙Yu$\n- $Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)⊙YX{X}^{T}$\n\n*Hint: Here, $\\text{diag}(v)$ is a diagonal matrix with the entries of $v$ on the diagonal.*" :question/worked-solution "The Hadamard product of two matrices (or vectors) is only defined if they have the same dimensions.\n\nFirst, notice that $X$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix, $Y$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, and $u$ is a $3\\times 1$ column vector.\n\nNow, let's consider each expression in turn:\n\n- The dimensions of $YX$ are $2\\times 2,$ and the dimensions of $Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)$ are $2\\times 3.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression I is *not* well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $X$ are $3\\times 2,$ and the dimensions of $Yu$ are $2\\times 1.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression II is *not* well-defined.\n\n- The dimensions of $Y\\,\\text{diag}(u)$ are $2\\times 3,$ and the dimensions of $YX{X}^{T}$ are $2\\times 3.$ Hence, the Hadamard product in expression III is well-defined.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186417378 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186115251 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" :knowledge-point/title "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05827898-9cd0-5481-9f4f-2795de94047f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186417360} {:answer/id #uuid "0fb31cbb-95b7-5502-b5a4-6f06608e01c1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id 17592186417361} {:answer/id #uuid "41d70487-8623-57c9-b21d-be91769cf161" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/id 17592186417366} {:answer/id #uuid "5daf2709-08dc-5216-87eb-2c674ea61e85" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id 17592186417368} {:answer/id #uuid "932f9073-db7f-59db-843a-495c4d7ae846" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id 17592186417372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05827898-9cd0-5481-9f4f-2795de94047f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id 17592186417360} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7d95426-738e-5629-870c-65da360e7800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186417377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b47d0cc7-71aa-58ba-a792-0650346f33fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-75505" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{5}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"."}] [{:db/id 17592186417602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c70d6a7a-298e-54bb-8a5d-dff2213b17bf" :knowledge-point/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Over Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23dee3e5-8fb2-530f-bfa9-3599d563e0d6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id 17592186417398} {:answer/id #uuid "2e24d934-08c4-58e3-be52-ce9f5121ebde" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id 17592186417408} {:answer/id #uuid "653569ac-ed4a-561b-b9be-9d858125d297" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id 17592186417436} {:answer/id #uuid "ead62325-324d-5dce-b93f-38dca6a0bf33" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id 17592186417531} {:answer/id #uuid "faab2d61-d391-5d78-81d3-04b266228963" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$, $S,$ and $T$" :db/id 17592186417541}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faab2d61-d391-5d78-81d3-04b266228963" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$, $S,$ and $T$" :db/id 17592186417541} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb7a1b4f-e571-5ec0-a3c9-9dbfbbd624fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186417590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16c25cec-ba90-5814-9c8d-34f1bb30c2b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-213599" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are symmetric? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x+y=0,$ defined on $R$\n\n- $X\\,T\\,Y$ if and only if $|X|=|Y|,$ defined on $P(N)$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ then $(y,x)\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\,⟹\\,y≡x\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in S$ then $(y,x)\\in S.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx+y=0\\,⟹\\,y+x=0\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in R.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(X,Y)\\in T$ then $(Y,X)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n|X|=|Y|\\,⟹\\,|Y|=|X|\n$$\n\nfor all $X,Y\\in P(N).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$, $S,$ and $T.$\""}] [{:db/id 17592186417608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12c5373d-0629-56d7-901e-7af879dd9ae2" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186417386} {:answer/id #uuid "1dc38fe7-4cc0-5b89-838a-99b8f54f414a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186417392} {:answer/id #uuid "32e8c8fd-be74-587d-a3b4-8214f956aaa8" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186417412} {:answer/id #uuid "8281f135-368b-5bd3-8ea5-ebad2352c486" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186417463} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7470ff-d87b-54d1-8fb8-68a8dbdf78fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186417488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dc38fe7-4cc0-5b89-838a-99b8f54f414a" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186417392} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a672eb5-09d4-52a3-990d-6a002fb2c6dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186417567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "84454995-d49e-5edd-80f6-dfbaab2558d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-213318" :question/problem "![](images/a8/a8ebf81e13d905d36997abf2f16c3732714b4feb56334f5e0c56443aa07732c6.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents an antisymmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $3\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 3$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 2$ and $2\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186417609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03d90713-bab4-5982-9789-c8022a2a41da" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186417380} {:answer/id #uuid "20bd4423-75c9-5c3e-b7e3-27c624187a6e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186417396} {:answer/id #uuid "353df1a9-635f-5637-a998-3ec12de5fb5d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186417414} {:answer/id #uuid "563b2cb6-9661-5e33-b3af-530622715f23" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186417430} {:answer/id #uuid "96510da6-be83-5df5-9b88-5179df3edb1f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186417474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "353df1a9-635f-5637-a998-3ec12de5fb5d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186417414} :answer-field/id #uuid "51bc889b-d302-50fa-aa28-ad159b53a97d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186417565}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "92e553e5-9efc-54d5-9f9a-59a998ba0a7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-213492" :question/problem "![](images/88/88e4ad0704f54f33241180593dc1ce4fe384710d5a680717f7c0449d5f85ec2c.png)\n\nWhich of the plots above represents a relation that is neither symmetric nor antisymmetric defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a plot, if a point $(x,y)$ belongs to the plot, then $(y,x),$ the point symmetric to $(x,y)$ over the diagonal line $y=x,$ should also be on the plot.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the points, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be on the plot of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows points on the diagonal line $y=x.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's draw the diagonal line $y=x$ and examine our plots.\n\n![](images/cd/cd4c4ac0b0b4c00e8cb29e8c95738d5c9c4404113c5370791e2277ca66cdbda2.png)\n\n \n\n- Plot I represents an antisymmetric relation since the plot contains at most one point from each pair of the points symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\n- Plot II represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since, for example, the pair $(a,b)$ belongs to the plot while the point $(b,a)$ does not. And it's not antisymmetric since it contains the pairs $(a,c)$ and $(c,a).$\n\n- Plot III represents a symmetric relation since the plot is symmetric over the line $y=x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186417612 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26015440-1e7f-5a41-a515-cdb226ffde44" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186417401} {:answer/id #uuid "71e3b3fd-c2f3-5398-bd50-93961d57fa71" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186417447} {:answer/id #uuid "88da9920-b611-5173-8083-4d93b60bb921" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186417466} {:answer/id #uuid "d873a68d-5dbd-5f44-ba5e-d5e3260cbab1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186417518} {:answer/id #uuid "e701ebf7-eb6f-578b-a734-909bfb567074" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186417528}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71e3b3fd-c2f3-5398-bd50-93961d57fa71" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186417447} :answer-field/id #uuid "e73c30be-aa49-512c-9991-68a5517212a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186417596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd780cfc-a5dd-5c8f-a2c7-e44b9d2a3075" :question/math-academy-id "q-213543" :question/problem "![](images/da/dac9030db59b2b51b2b95c8bc9b5988106be8d198b7f947d33c8678d1cfa3b7b.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a symmetric relation defined on the set $A={1,2,3}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph II represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph III represents neither a symmetric nor an antisymmetric relation. It's not symmetric since the arrow $1\\to 2$ belongs to the digraph while the arrow $2\\to 1$ does not. It is not antisymmetric since, for example, arrows $1\\to 3$ and $3\\to 1$ belong to the digraph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186417615 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203926 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4dee31d-47c2-5b56-8cea-22d42f7b9de2" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric and Antisymmetric Relations From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cfbb87-25ee-57d3-9593-65b9056166c3" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186417413} {:answer/id #uuid "681eaafc-b181-55c0-a5c1-50fc24ec41d4" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186417438} {:answer/id #uuid "b65b6025-f874-5bd7-abde-81594d01d6e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186417491} {:answer/id #uuid "d0a402d1-6d3b-5a39-88ac-48a25a79c7ba" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186417509} {:answer/id #uuid "f66b2634-5d28-5976-9c01-6e76341a26f6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186417540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b65b6025-f874-5bd7-abde-81594d01d6e6" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186417491} :answer-field/id #uuid "c65f5ba4-1fea-57cb-bbe2-73b81c041018" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186417587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ee6fe66d-09a5-518d-9a32-a533c9b3cc75" :question/math-academy-id "q-213540" :question/problem "![](images/70/70743ac83b876995835703b35fc133af2133960ea916f589d60c8ac5636ee4ef.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a symmetric relation defined on the set $A={a,b,c}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have an arrow $x\\to y,$ then the arrow $y\\to x$ must be in the digraph too.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nSo, only one of the arrows, either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ can be in the digraph of an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows loops.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\n- Digraph II represents an antisymmetric relation since for all possible $x$ and $y$ with $x\\ne y,$ *at most one* of the arrows (either $x\\to y$ or $y\\to x,$ but not both) belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III represents a symmetric relation since for every arrow $x\\to y$ belonging to the graph, the corresponding inverse arrow $y\\to x$ also belongs to the graph.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186417616 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203927 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c70d6a7a-298e-54bb-8a5d-dff2213b17bf" :knowledge-point/title "Symmetric and Antisymmetric Over Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19eae037-9bb3-53cd-9405-bcdcf90085f1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $S$ only" :db/id 17592186417390} {:answer/id #uuid "1fc5dc30-0af1-522a-98a7-9dc7cae28dc1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id 17592186417393} {:answer/id #uuid "30083d0d-2315-5a99-8f56-85fda87401af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id 17592186417409} {:answer/id #uuid "82cc221d-48ae-5487-9285-67993b828548" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id 17592186417464} {:answer/id #uuid "c5013afa-3560-51a7-8ce8-80f8c3522c62" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id 17592186417504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30083d0d-2315-5a99-8f56-85fda87401af" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id 17592186417409} :answer-field/id #uuid "b27f213d-f5cd-55a6-989e-1054aab40f4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186417580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe8a5755-a006-512f-a829-ac55db45a0f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-213600" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are symmetric? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $y={x}^{2},$ defined on $R$\n\n- $X\\,S\\,Y$ if and only if $X⊂Y,$ defined on $P(R)$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $4∣(x+y),$ defined on $N$" :question/worked-solution "First, recall the following definitions:\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *symmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(y,x)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if a pair $(x,y)$ belongs to a symmetric relation, then the pair $(y,x)$ should also be in the relation.\n\n- A binary relation $R,$ defined on a set $A,$ is *antisymmetric* if for all $x,y\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,x)\\in R\\,⟹\\,x=y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, only one of the pairs, either $(x,y)$ or $(y,x),$ can be in an antisymmetric relation. Note that this condition allows pairs of the form $(x,x).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is not symmetric. Notice that \n\n- $(-1,1)\\in R$ since $1=(-1)^{2},$ while\n\n- $(1,-1)\\notin R$ since $-1\\ne {1}^{2}.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not symmetric. Notice that \n\n- $({1},Z)\\in S$ since ${1}⊂Z,$ while\n\n- $(Z,{1})\\notin S$ since $Z⊄{1}.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is symmetric. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in T$ then $(y,x)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\n4∣(x+y)\\,⟹\\,4∣(y+x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y\\in N.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186417704 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124631 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "113a601e-220a-54a3-a64b-1e554bc3d79f" :knowledge-point/title "Сompleting a Sentence Using the Invertible Matrix Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f10238c-7d05-58b9-96e8-9c69a8801161" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186417685} {:answer/id #uuid "b1eb1104-92b0-5dcf-bd12-07d9a0132bf5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186417691} {:answer/id #uuid "d5329d0a-dafa-5056-a8ca-478564ecacb5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186417693} {:answer/id #uuid "eeaaa408-6e32-550a-b040-2adb608fe8fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186417696} {:answer/id #uuid "fb5e2579-8b62-5595-a1e0-8ecfe4516933" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186417698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeaaa408-6e32-550a-b040-2adb608fe8fc" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186417696} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b17b4e5-ae42-5c71-8493-ac975ab35b5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186417702}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c77aeb41-578d-533f-87d1-e33216f35c1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-84053" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T$ and ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}.$  Consider the following statement:\n\n*If ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\ne 0$ and $T({u}_{1})-T({u}_{2})=0,$ then $\\text{__________}.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$\n- $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$\n- $T$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $T$ is a linear transformation and $T({u}_{1})-T({u}_{2})=0,$ we have $T({u}_{1}-{u}_{2})=0.$ So ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and ${u}_{1}-{u}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Therefore, we must have $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$, then $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\n- Statement III is false. According to the invertible matrix theorem, since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3,$ the transformation $T$ can't be invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186417815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0972b4fe-a6de-5b28-91e1-b8f15d278acf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186417785} {:answer/id #uuid "26c9aa3e-4575-55fe-bef6-0e504f1fb60e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186417789} {:answer/id #uuid "3fd3bade-13d7-5747-b1e8-adac5e633beb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186417792} {:answer/id #uuid "505ee955-4b02-50a3-9047-a855589dfea1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186417796} {:answer/id #uuid "b7801bcd-fba9-5c75-b2ca-bfd94012877d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186417803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26c9aa3e-4575-55fe-bef6-0e504f1fb60e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186417789} :answer-field/id #uuid "b71b8e04-6ae9-5c68-998d-3aaf22c66038" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186417813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4db015a0-682b-5ff4-909b-ad19f05ab168" :question/math-academy-id "q-71826" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b838b945d0b4002d8ae2238af5992c83dcf23b2985c448c716a5caf8de31b369.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/be/be1ece00243777f3f6b6973319e15ea1e5c8556b2452b61a451713f9dcb86595.png)\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is false. The point $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I only'."}] [{:db/id 17592186417818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186124634 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69b456ea-44de-59f0-b2c8-bc5b9495974f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements From a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e33f924-4eb3-55c2-bd43-bfeaf2283adf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186417790} {:answer/id #uuid "9d0ca9a9-7bc2-5a65-be19-0f409201b0c9" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186417799} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b376f2-838b-5270-8108-30de4fdb1fcb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186417804} {:answer/id #uuid "f9e22708-5653-5d53-b84a-eb8c3f50f2b7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186417807} {:answer/id #uuid "fc4df2d7-22d1-55de-8deb-c49dbe88df60" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186417808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1b376f2-838b-5270-8108-30de4fdb1fcb" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id 17592186417804} :answer-field/id #uuid "24ed1e3e-e1e8-525a-96ba-0dbe6634885b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186417810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7105850-e717-5535-8a28-db3aaf9f3031" :question/math-academy-id "q-159495" :question/problem "![](images/19/19174ec33e43c900773031043d5de200e892f5e3ee51704d0800b6a944147155.png)\n\nConsider the set $M,$ depicted by the shaded region above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A$ is an interior point of the set $M$\n- $B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$\n- $C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/43/43362800e91cbb57c22fc6851c10f00927a030a790dea9284e69a5c7c3d4ae25.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'I, II, and III'."}] [{:db/id 17592186418065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186172049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "337518a9-39a1-57e3-8686-74a135f7c3d7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Oriented Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b798def-9f4c-5c78-939d-4d3fa7b155b5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186418054} {:answer/id #uuid "36a1e53d-4736-58e7-9183-e39dca9e0ce1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id 17592186418055} {:answer/id #uuid "4305aa10-6b9d-5a8d-b5e0-73d08076c6a1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id 17592186418056} {:answer/id #uuid "64b20a39-4422-555e-853c-addaea9024df" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186418058} {:answer/id #uuid "6db79444-4f16-52ba-8432-cedcce75ea2c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II, III, and IV only" :db/id 17592186418059}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b798def-9f4c-5c78-939d-4d3fa7b155b5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186418054} :answer-field/id #uuid "8230f704-b679-555a-b3ea-6455f032cf02" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186418063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d6641b4f-e396-5ff5-8410-bd9f53c4e8d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-98339" :question/problem "![](images/98/9836234d763ccb3da6b9ac0360dcb3662cacc5fc90d5ecc2dee20bf29ce0c77d.png)\n\nWhich of the curves above is simple, closed, and negatively oriented?" :question/worked-solution "A simple closed curve is positively oriented if, when traveling along it, one always has the curve interior to the left and the curve exterior to the right. Otherwise, the curve is negatively oriented. \n\nA curve is positively oriented if it is traversed counterclockwise and is negatively oriented if it is traversed clockwise.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the curves:\n\n- Curve I is neither simple nor closed.\n\n- Curve II is a simple and closed curve but it is not negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed counterclockwise, so it is positively oriented.\n\n- Curve III is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\n- Curve IV is simple, closed, negatively oriented. The arrows indicate that the curve is traversed clockwise.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III and IV only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186418243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "630cd12d-1141-51ae-8f2e-a881622868e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations in Special Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2571c089-3c52-59d7-b473-6f9e0305f21e" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id 17592186418167} {:answer/id #uuid "2f967206-082f-5b15-9867-3bb8d435bad0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186418170} {:answer/id #uuid "ac9e3534-044f-5762-90a9-b43cb8e6b482" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186418201} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9cc960-4433-54c6-935f-f6a98ffc3777" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186418213} {:answer/id #uuid "db2ad807-5ae0-5e39-8f4e-ad108c836dcd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186418215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f967206-082f-5b15-9867-3bb8d435bad0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186418170} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8488c7a-8619-58cd-a22e-a5aa6766ebf6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186418238}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "202c6abf-dfb5-522a-9256-456835ce12bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-214430" :question/problem "Which of the following relations on the set $A={1,3,5,7}$ are transitive?\n\n- ${R}_{1}=∅$\n- ${R}_{2}={A}^{2}$\n- ${R}_{3}={(5,5)}$\n- ${R}_{4}={(5,1),(7,3)}$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R={A}^{2}$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R=∅$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- ${R}_{1}$ is transitive since it's the empty relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{2}$ is transitive since it's the universal relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{3}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{4}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186418244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203886 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "630cd12d-1141-51ae-8f2e-a881622868e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations in Special Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "344c8bff-5d19-596d-a2bf-472abe96d0ef" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III only" :db/id 17592186418172} {:answer/id #uuid "5b9a150a-cc66-599e-963e-2b2c4bed2295" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and IV only" :db/id 17592186418181} {:answer/id #uuid "62c920c8-4114-5cee-ab28-e34c74683438" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186418184} {:answer/id #uuid "b26882ef-7ad2-53e9-a273-98e2183e198c" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186418203} {:answer/id #uuid "bf74cb06-4c45-5759-8ad8-f5a6d7a6e7f5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186418206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf74cb06-4c45-5759-8ad8-f5a6d7a6e7f5" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, III, and IV" :db/id 17592186418206} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ba6de5b-5a73-5a97-aff3-85fcde63e486" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186418223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44cd3428-3b1c-5e19-9525-5e86c3ca616a" :question/math-academy-id "q-214431" :question/problem "Which of the following relations on the set $A={♢,♡,♠,♣}$ are transitive?\n\n- ${R}_{1}={(♢,♢),(♣,♣)}$\n- ${R}_{2}={(♠,♠)}$\n- ${R}_{3}={(♢,♠),(♡,♣)}$\n- ${R}_{4}=∅$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z:$\n\nMoreover, we recall the following:\n\n- The universal relation $R={A}^{2}$ is transitive since it includes all possible ordered pairs.\n\n- The empty relation $R=∅$ is vacuously transitive since the antecedent in the implication is always false, and therefore the implication is always true.\n\n- A relation containing no \"linked\" pairs is always transitive.\n\n- A relation containing only reflexive pairs is always transitive.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations:\n\n- ${R}_{1}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{2}$ is transitive since it contains only reflexive pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{3}$ is transitive since it contains no \"linked\" pairs.  $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${R}_{4}$ is transitive since it's the empty relation. $\\,\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186418246 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203887 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f65dc026-1ec7-51f3-abc2-4a8ce95f9c2c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Transitive Relations From a Digraph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e69dd30-577b-5eb0-8abc-73d0336ecd72" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186418179} {:answer/id #uuid "81acb1cb-213f-53e4-b5af-c60b8dbbec53" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186418188} {:answer/id #uuid "9bc6d790-c4ad-5910-942b-7e8326e0da7d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186418194} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c59538-8b1e-5b20-9482-690ba54c57e0" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186418199} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c12167-746e-5442-b291-b883d8a00d11" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186418210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9bc6d790-c4ad-5910-942b-7e8326e0da7d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186418194} :answer-field/id #uuid "18915ff6-afcd-56a1-8b11-45e467d4f091" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186418226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64ea6724-f6be-5851-a399-860e0e1924d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-214065" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1fa323b54736f62bb334ae491d8d41deeba4a4e325f6e56f9651da147d50c349.png)\n\nWhich of the above digraphs represents a transitive relation defined on the set $A={p,q,r}?$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nIn terms of a digraph, if we have \"linked\" arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ then the arrow $x\\to z$ must be in the digraph too.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our digraphs.\n\n- Digraph I does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $q\\to r$ and $r\\to p,$ but has no arrow $q\\to p.$\n\n- Digraph II represents a transitive relation. For any pair of arrows $x\\to y$ and $y\\to z,$ the arrow  $x\\to z$ also belongs to the digraph.\n\n- Digraph III does not represent a transitive relation. It has arrows $p\\to q$ and $q\\to r,$ but has no arrow $p\\to r.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186418247 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186203888 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "947f058c-4c47-52df-8c28-aed016d2b148" :knowledge-point/title "Transitive Relations Defined On Infinite Sets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c5a523e-87ad-564c-a53c-0f5976b548cd" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ and $T$ only" :db/id 17592186418195} {:answer/id #uuid "c19f5713-624c-5de7-b871-ec4d7fff4f4f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$S$ only" :db/id 17592186418207} {:answer/id #uuid "c9cc9a32-4374-5c62-b18b-114a993cc029" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ only" :db/id 17592186418211} {:answer/id #uuid "e44c0d0e-d054-5dd5-8926-ef5982032103" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id 17592186418217} {:answer/id #uuid "e564fc12-fb2c-519f-9637-195e019015f7" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ only" :db/id 17592186418218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e44c0d0e-d054-5dd5-8926-ef5982032103" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R$ and $T$ only" :db/id 17592186418217} :answer-field/id #uuid "edb2118f-fc60-559a-b8bd-52ea337c03d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186418240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70eb9024-0964-59ad-a103-f6948d25a78c" :question/math-academy-id "q-214002" :question/problem "Which of the following relations are transitive? \n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y,$ defined on $Z$\n\n- $x\\,S\\,y$ if and only if $x+y<4,$ defined on $R$\n\n- $x\\,T\\,y$ if and only if $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4),$ defined on $Z$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation $R$ defined on a set $A$ is *transitive* if, for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\,\\text{and}\\,(y,z)\\in R\\,⟹\\,(x,z)\\in R.\n$$\n\nIn other words, if the pairs $(x,y)$ and $(y,z)$ belong to a transitive relation, then the pair $(x,z)$ should also be in the relation.\n\nIt's sufficient to check all the \"linked\" pairs of the form $(x,y)$ with $x\\ne y$ and $(y,z)$ with $y\\ne z.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our relations.\n\n- Relation $R$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,x)\\in R,$ then $(x,z)\\in R.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx∣y\\,\\text{and}\\,y∣z\\,⟹\\,x∣z\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z\\in Z.$\n\n- Relation $S$ is not transitive. Notice that \n\n- $(1,-4)\\in S$ since $1+(-4)=-3<4,$ and\n\n- $(-4,5)\\in S$ since $-4+5=1<4,$ but\n\n- $(1,5)\\notin S$ since $1+5=6≮4.$\n\n- Relation $T$ is transitive. Indeed, if $(x,y)\\in T$ and $(y,x)\\in T,$ then $(x,z)\\in T.$ Notice that \n\n$$\nx≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,\\text{and}\\,y≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\\,⟹\\,x≡z\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)\n$$\n\nfor all $x,y,z\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$R$ and $T$ only.\""}] [{:db/id 17592186419533 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id 17592186157308 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f0a57c2-bbad-5225-976b-385c7f117738" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Poisson Distribution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15725fb5-a87c-5f38-b289-86f0d29427bf" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id 17592186419436} {:answer/id #uuid "1c6398d0-50af-5610-9bee-df1bd0a9db26" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id 17592186419438} {:answer/id #uuid "8b67915e-f629-5f65-a339-cb1785e1012d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186419469} {:answer/id #uuid "9f745b3a-8022-5a3b-be01-5e2c7e0f5ca1" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id 17592186419478} {:answer/id #uuid "bcbe5d78-91aa-5b37-8eb4-0ae8d7b2f02f" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id 17592186419486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b67915e-f629-5f65-a339-cb1785e1012d" :answer/type {:db/id 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id 17592186419469} :answer-field/id #uuid "11088ee4-ce12-5820-98c9-e84060e47cf8" :answer-fi